meta {
  name: Start playing
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}{{apiPrefix}}/me/games/{{gameId}}/sessions
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "startDate": "2026-08-18"
  }
}

docs {
  Opens a play session. No `endDate` means an OPEN session, which is what makes the
  game "currently playing"; there is no boolean column that could disagree.

  Returns the GAME with fresh play state, not the created session. The session is
  not a resource any client reads on its own: every consumer wants the five derived
  fields that hang off the game. That saves a refetch at the cost of a 201 whose
  body is not the thing that was created.

  Dates default to the server clock (UTC in production), so the web UI always sends
  the browser-local date.

  - 201 the game, now currently playing
  - 404 game nonexistent or someone else's
  - 409 the game already has an open session
  - 422 endDate before startDate
  - 429 over the write budget
}
